+## 30. Locked comparison to R LTFHPlus (`bench_ltfhplus_compare.py`)
+
+Same classic LT-FH families and bounds, three independent cohorts of 200
+nuclear pedigrees (parents + one sibling), h² = 0.5, K = 0.05, tol = 0.01,
+n_sim = 100,000, burn_in = 1,000 — LTFHPlus 2.2.0's own Gibbs settings.
+LTFHPlus is Gibbs-only. Pearson–Aitken is ltpred-only. `±` is the
+across-replicate SE. Numba used 4 threads; LTFHPlus used 1 `future` worker
+(its default sequential plan). A 4-worker LTFHPlus repeat on the same
+design was not faster (15.0 ± 1.3 s vs 13.2 ± 1.2 s): session startup
+dominated.
+
+| Estimator | corr vs LTFHPlus | RMSE vs LTFHPlus | seconds / 200 families |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| LTFHPlus Gibbs | — | — | 13.21 ± 1.17 |
+| ltpred Gibbs | 0.9999 ± 0.0000 | 0.0050 ± 0.0001 | 2.69 ± 0.14 |
+| ltpred PA | 0.9999 ± 0.0000 | 0.0045 ± 0.0002 | 0.0032 ± 0.0006 |
+
+The scores agree. The residual RMSE is at the scale of the Gibbs Monte
+Carlo error (LTFHPlus reports `genetic_se` ≈ 0.004). This is a numerical
+lock of the no-mixture Gibbs path, not of the PA-FGRS mixture.
+
+Wall-clock on this machine: LTFHPlus / ltpred Gibbs = 4.9; LTFHPlus /
+ltpred PA = 4.1×10³. The first ratio is Gibbs versus Gibbs across
+language and parallelisation. The second mixes algorithms: PA is the
+sequential two-moment approximation, not a faster Gibbs. Neither ratio
+is a hardware-independent constant. Opt-in: requires R and LTFHPlus;
+the script exits 2 if they are missing.
+
