+\subsection{Public LTFHPlus}
+\label{sec:ltfhplus}
+
+The same classic LT-FH rectangles, three cohorts of $200$ nuclear
+families, $\h=0.5$, $K=0.05$, and LTFHPlus 2.2.0's own Gibbs
+settings ($n_{\mathrm{sim}}=10^{5}$, burn-in $1000$,
+$\mathrm{tol}=0.01$).
+LTFHPlus is Gibbs-only.
+Table~\ref{tab:ltfhplus} is the lock.
+
+\begin{table}[ht]
+\centering
+\caption{Same families and bounds.
+LTFHPlus used one \texttt{future} worker; ltpred Gibbs used four
+Numba threads.
+RMSE is against the R scores.}
+\label{tab:ltfhplus}
+\small
+\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
+\toprule
+Estimator & corr vs LTFHPlus & RMSE & seconds / $200$ families \\
+\midrule
+LTFHPlus Gibbs
+  & --- & --- & $13.21\pm 1.17$ \\
+ltpred Gibbs
+  & $0.9999\pm 0.0000$ & $0.0050\pm 0.0001$
+  & $2.69\pm 0.14$ \\
+ltpred PA
+  & $0.9999\pm 0.0000$ & $0.0045\pm 0.0002$
+  & $0.0032\pm 0.0006$ \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{table}
+
+The scores agree at the scale of the Gibbs Monte Carlo error.
+On this machine one-worker LTFHPlus took $4.9\times$ as long as
+four-thread ltpred Gibbs.
+PA versus LTFHPlus is a different algorithm, about
+$4{\times}10^{3}$ on this run, not ``LT-FH++, but faster.''
+The mixture \eqref{eq:mix} is not in this comparison.
+
